WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.068 --> 00:00:04.424
The policeman on the beat moved up the avenue impressively.

2
00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:09.906
The impressiveness was habitual and not for show, for spectators were few.

3
00:00:09.907 --> 00:00:19.301
The time was barely ten o'clock at night, but chilly gusts of wind with a taste of rain in them had well-nigh depeopled the streets.

4
00:00:19.302 --> 00:00:37.520
Trying doors as he went, twirling his club with many intricate and artful movements, turning now and then to cast his watchful eye down the pacific thoroughfare, the officer, with his stalwart form and slight swagger, made a fine picture of a guardian of the peace.

5
00:00:37.521 --> 00:00:40.865
The vicinity was one that kept early hours.

6
00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:51.843
Now and then, you might see the lights of a cigar store or of an all-night lunch counter; but the majority of the doors belonged to business places that had long since been closed.

7
00:00:51.844 --> 00:00:57.833
When about midway of a certain block, the policeman suddenly slowed his walk.

8
00:00:57.834 --> 00:01:05.066
In the doorway of a darkened hardware store, a man leaned, with an unlighted cigar in his mouth.

9
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:09.141
As the policeman walked up to him, the man spoke up quickly.

10
00:01:09.142 --> 00:01:14.210
"It's all right, officer," he said reassuringly. "I'm just waiting for a friend.

11
00:01:14.211 --> 00:01:19.184
It's an appointment made twenty years ago. Sounds a little funny to you, doesn't it?

12
00:01:19.185 --> 00:01:22.525
Well, I'll explain if you'd like to make certain it's all straight.

13
00:01:22.526 --> 00:01:29.167
About that long ago, there used to be a restaurant where this store stands—‘Big Joe’ Brady's restaurant."

14
00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:34.389
"Until five years ago," said the policeman. "It was torn down then."

15
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.219
The man in the doorway struck a match and lit his cigar.

16
00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:45.735
The light showed a pale, square-jawed face with keen eyes and a little white scar near his right eyebrow.

17
00:01:45.736 --> 00:01:50.184
His scarfpin was a large diamond, oddly set.

18
00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:58.916
"Twenty years ago tonight," said the man, "I dined here at 'Big Joe' Brady's with Jimmy Wells, my best chum and the finest chap in the world.

19
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:06.056
He and I were raised here in New York, just like two brothers, together. I was eighteen and Jimmy was twenty.

20
00:02:06.057 --> 00:02:10.436
The next morning, I was to start for the West to make my fortune.

21
00:02:10.437 --> 00:02:15.402
You couldn't have dragged Jimmy out of New York; he thought it was the only place on earth.

22
00:02:15.403 --> 00:02:27.136
Well, we agreed that night that we would meet here again exactly twenty years from that date and time, no matter what our conditions might be or from what distance we might have to come.

23
00:02:27.137 --> 00:02:34.635
We figured that in twenty years each of us ought to have our destiny worked out and our fortunes made, whatever they were going to be."

24
00:02:34.636 --> 00:02:38.140
"It sounds pretty interesting," said the policeman.

25
00:02:38.141 --> 00:02:44.894
"Rather a long time between meets, though, it seems to me. Haven't you heard from your friend since you left?"

26
00:02:44.895 --> 00:02:49.124
"Well, yes, for a time we corresponded," said the other.

27
00:02:49.125 --> 00:02:59.869
"But after a year or two, we lost track of each other. You see, the West is a pretty big proposition, and I kept hustling around over it pretty lively.

28
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:07.806
But I know Jimmy will meet me here if he's alive, for he always was the truest, staunchest old chap in the world.

29
00:03:07.807 --> 00:03:15.623
He'll never forget. I came a thousand miles to stand in this door tonight, and it's worth it if my old partner turns up."

30
00:03:15.624 --> 00:03:21.478
The waiting man pulled out a handsome watch, the lids of it set with small diamonds.

31
00:03:21.479 --> 00:03:28.406
"Three minutes to ten," he announced. "It was exactly ten o'clock when we parted here at the restaurant door."

32
00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:32.912
"Did pretty well out West, didn't you?" asked the policeman.

33
00:03:32.913 --> 00:03:40.858
"You bet! I hope Jimmy has done half as well. He was a kind of plodder, though, good fellow as he was.

34
00:03:40.859 --> 00:03:45.402
I've had to compete with some of the sharpest wits going to get my pile.

35
00:03:45.403 --> 00:03:50.976
A man gets in a groove in New York. It takes the West to put a razor edge on

36
00:03:50.977 --> 00:03:54.571
him." The policeman twirled his club and took a step or

37
00:03:54.572 --> 00:04:00.624
two. "I'll be on my way. Hope your friend comes around all right. Going to call time on him

38
00:04:00.625 --> 00:04:03.203
sharp?" "I should say not!" said the

39
00:04:03.204 --> 00:04:11.133
other. "I'll give him half an hour at least. If Jimmy is alive on earth, he'll be here by that time. So long,

40
00:04:11.134 --> 00:04:17.942
officer." "Good night, sir," said the policeman, passing on along his beat, trying doors as he

41
00:04:17.943 --> 00:04:26.332
went. There was now a fine, cold drizzle falling, and the wind had risen from its uncertain puffs into a steady

42
00:04:26.333 --> 00:04:35.636
blow. The few foot passengers in that quarter hurried dismally and silently along with coat collars turned high and pocketed

43
00:04:35.637 --> 00:04:48.165
hands. And in the door of the hardware store, the man who had come a thousand miles to fill an appointment, uncertain almost to absurdity, with the friend of his youth, smoked his cigar and

44
00:04:48.166 --> 00:04:58.827
waited. About twenty minutes he waited, and then a tall man in a long overcoat, with collar turned up to his ears, hurried across from the opposite side of the

45
00:04:58.828 --> 00:05:01.332
street. He went directly to the waiting

46
00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:04.640
man. "Is that you, Bob?" he asked

47
00:05:04.641 --> 00:05:08.379
doubtfully. "Is that you, Jimmy Wells?" cried the man in the

48
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:14.313
door. "Bless my heart!" exclaimed the new arrival, grasping both the other's hands with his

49
00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:20.469
own. "It's Bob, sure as fate. I was certain I'd find you here if you were still in

50
00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.231
existence. Well, well, well! Twenty years is a long

51
00:05:24.232 --> 00:05:30.029
time. The old restaurant's gone, Bob; I wish it had lasted, so we could have had another dinner

52
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.941
there. How has the West treated you, old

53
00:05:32.942 --> 00:05:41.730
man?" "Bully; it has given me everything I asked it for. You've changed lots, Jimmy. I never thought you were so tall by two or three

54
00:05:41.731 --> 00:05:44.309
inches." "Oh, I grew a bit after I was

55
00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.251
twenty." "Doing well in New York,

56
00:05:46.252 --> 00:05:50.295
Jimmy?" "Moderately. I have a position in one of the city

57
00:05:50.296 --> 00:05:56.328
departments. Come on, Bob; we'll go around to a place I know of, and have a good long talk about old

58
00:05:56.329 --> 00:05:59.790
times." The two men started up the street, arm in

59
00:05:59.791 --> 00:06:06.979
arm. The man from the West, his egotism enlarged by success, was beginning to outline the history of his

60
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:11.961
career. The other, submerged in his overcoat, listened with

61
00:06:11.962 --> 00:06:16.577
interest. At the corner stood a drug store, brilliant with electric

62
00:06:16.578 --> 00:06:23.019
lights. When they came into this glare, each of them turned simultaneously to gaze upon the other's

63
00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.519
face. The man from the West stopped suddenly and released his

64
00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:36.390
arm. "You're not Jimmy Wells," he snapped. "Twenty years is a long time, but not long enough to change a man's nose from a Roman to a

65
00:06:36.391 --> 00:06:41.953
pug." "It sometimes changes a good man into a bad one," said the tall

66
00:06:41.954 --> 00:06:46.032
man. "You've been under arrest for ten minutes, 'Silky'

67
00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:51.493
Bob. Chicago thinks you may have dropped over our way and wires us she wants to have a chat with

68
00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:55.165
you. Going quietly, are you? That's

69
00:06:55.166 --> 00:07:05.174
sensible. Now, before we go on to the station, here's a note I was asked to hand you. You may read it here at the window. It's from Patrolman

70
00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:09.560
Wells." The man from the West unfolded the little piece of paper handed

71
00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:17.530
him. His hand was steady when he began to read, but it trembled a little by the time he had finished. The note was rather

72
00:07:17.531 --> 00:07:21.117
short. Bob: I was at the appointed place on

73
00:07:21.118 --> 00:07:27.174
time. When you struck the match to light your cigar, I saw it was the face of the man wanted in

74
00:07:27.183 --> 00:07:33.789
Chicago. Somehow, I couldn’t do it myself, so I went around and got a plainclothes man to do the

75
00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:34.804
job. Jimmy

